From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: sun60i-a733: recover the root port after a link-down reset

[Recipe cut: regenerated: context only, the removed stop_after_link
parameter is not touched here.]

After the link drops, the root port is wiped and nothing puts it back.
Measured on the 6 GB board on 2026-09-21 (kernel with `pci-sun60i-a733-root-port-event-irq-on-sii` and `pci-sun60i-a733-remove-root-port-vpd-capability`), read right
after a link-down during the preset sweep, no reboot in between:

  SII_INT_1 (UDS+0x0e0c)  0x0200001F   bit 3 LINK_REQ_RST_NOT latched
  root port COMMAND 0000, bus numbers 00000000, LnkCtl 0000, RtCtl 0000,
            AER Root Error Command 00000000
  DBI 0x820 (PCIE_MSI_ADDR_LO) still 0x4207E000
  root port runtime_suspended_time 0, aerdrv thread idle, no AER recovery
  or hung-task line

So it was neither runtime PM nor a stuck AER recovery: the controller reset
itself. The UM describes exactly that. Under PCIE_LTSSM_ENABLE (V1.00
18.3.8.55, printed p. 1221), the hot-reset / link-down-reset entry: the
controller requests a reset on smlh/link_req_rst_not and then resets itself,
leaving the sticky registers alone. LINK_REQ_RST_NOT in SII_INT_1 (18.3.8.63,
p. 1240) flags that request, raised by a link-down or by a received
hot-reset request. Config space is non-sticky and goes; the port
logic survives, as the MSI target shows. The drive stays unreachable and
AER, PME and bwctrl stay deaf until reboot.

THE FIX, ALIGNED WITH MAINLINE

Mainline has since gained a mechanism for exactly this case: `pci-err-reset-root-ports-platform-specific` and `pci-host-common-link-down-handling-for-root-ports`
backport pci_host_bridge::reset_root_port and pci_host_handle_link_down().
This patch is a user of it, shaped like the upstream pcie-dw-rockchip and
pcie-qcom users:

 - SII_INT_MASK_1 bit 3 unmasks LINK_REQ_RST_NOT onto the SII line that `pci-sun60i-a733-root-port-event-irq-on-sii`
   already chains. The handler disarms it and queues a work item on
   system_long_wq; the work calls pci_host_handle_link_down() for the root
   port. Armed in post_init after enumeration, and only over a trained link:
   during the first training the request is legitimately active, and with
   no device there is nothing to recover ("no link: link-down handling
   off").
 - Arming clears the latch, which would erase a drop latched while
   disarmed; the glue's reset and the retrain after it need no software, so
   such a drop leaves a trained link over a wiped port. So every arm is
   followed by a canary: the secondary bus number, which probe, setup and
   restore leave non-zero and the controller's reset clears to 0 (measured).
   A zero starts the recovery the latch would have, and says so.
 - A budget: 3 recoveries per 60 s, then link-down handling stays off until
   reboot, with an error. dw_pcie_setup_rc() ends with a directed speed
   change, so a recovered link retrains to Gen3 and equalizes again; on a
   channel that fails that the recovery would otherwise loop.
 - pci_host_handle_link_down() freezes the drivers below the port
   (pcie_do_recovery(), pci_channel_io_frozen), resets the port through
   pci_bus_error_reset() -> pcibios_reset_secondary_bus() -> our
   reset_root_port(), then restores the port's config space from the state
   saved at enumeration (pci_restore_state()), waits for the drive
   (pci_bridge_wait_for_secondary_bus()), and calls the drivers' slot_reset
   and resume.
 - reset_root_port() holds the LTSSM, pulses PERST#, releases the LTSSM,
   waits for Data Link Layer Link Active (read through the DBI; the SII
   link-up bits are W1C and may only latch), and only then runs the
   `pci-sun60i-a733-root-port-event-irq-on-sii` and `pci-sun60i-a733-remove-root-port-vpd-capability` capability unlinks and dw_pcie_setup_rc(). Not the other way
   round: holding app_ltssm_enable here is the UM's first hot-reset window
   (18.3.8.55: a DBI read, then app_ltssm_enable = 1, then the
   controller reset starts), so DBI writes made while it is held come
   before that reset. Measured on 2026-09-22, same Link Disable stimulus:
   with the setup inside the hold the link came back only through the
   core's 2.5 GT/s retrain (pcie_failed_link_retrain(), "broken device,
   retraining non-functional downstream link at 2.5GT/s") and stayed at
   2.5 GT/s, 192 MB/s; with the setup after the release it came back at
   8 GT/s, 614 MB/s. The upstream rockchip/qcom callbacks write the setup
   while the LTSSM is held because they reset the core themselves first;
   here the controller does. The setup's directed speed change takes the
   link back to 8 GT/s. The canary after the setup catches a controller
   reset that came after it, and the setup runs again. The link wait has
   dw_pcie_wait_for_link()'s budget (900 ms) and only informs: after the
   PERST# pulse the drive took longer than that in both orders. The wait
   that decides is the core's pci_bridge_wait_for_secondary_bus(), after
   pci_restore_state(). The UM does not say whether the reset restores
   the capability pointers the DBI rewrote; the unlink logs if it found a
   capability back, and that line is the measurement. Last, the handler
   is re-armed (with the canary) if the link is up; otherwise the work
   item arms it after the core's wait.
 - The same callback serves every reset of the port's bus, including AER
   fatal recovery and a bus reset requested for the drive. A plain
   Secondary Bus Reset would drop the link and wipe the port in the middle
   of it. That is the reason for one PERST#-based sequence instead of
   trusting the link drop.

How NVMe sees it: error_detected(frozen) disables the controller and asks
for a reset; slot_reset restores its config space (the drive lost its BARs
with the link) and schedules a controller reset; resume waits for it. The
queues come back without a rescan. If the drive does not answer within the
core's wait, recovery fails and the NVMe driver removes the controller:
"device recovery failed", the same end as today, but with a root port that
is programmed again.

TEGRA194, READ AND NOT COPIED

pcie-tegra194.c:374 handles LINK_REQ_RST_NOT too. Taken from it: this signal
is the one to act on, in the controller's own interrupt. Rejected:
 - its CAR_RESET_OVRD pulse of core_rst_n, a Tegra register with no A733
   counterpart. The A733 glue already resets the controller by itself
   (measured above).
 - its PORT_LOGIC_SPEED_CHANGE kick in hard-IRQ context. That is a Tegra
   workaround for a missing SBR fix (has_sbr_reset_fix), and it restores
   nothing. A retrain from an IRQ is not a recovery.
 - handling in the hard IRQ at all. The recovery sleeps (PERST#, link wait,
   device wait) and must freeze the drivers first.

DELIBERATELY NOT DONE

 - The UM's alternative of delaying the controller reset (clear
   app_ltssm_enable combinationally, in the same instant
   as the falling edge of smlh_req_rst_not) so registers can be saved first.
   Software cannot react within that edge; the state saved at enumeration is what mainline
   restores too.
 - Re-arming after a recovery that left the link down. The request would
   fire again at once; the log says the handling is disarmed.

VERIFIED

Applies at fuzz 0 after `pci-err-reset-root-ports-platform-specific` and `pci-host-common-link-down-handling-for-root-ports` and builds (compile-check). The driver and
drivers/pci/pci.o, pcie/err.o, pcie/aer.o and controller/pci-host-common.o
also build with W=1 without warnings; the plain compile check does not build the latter.
CONFIG_PCI_HOST_COMMON=y through the new select. The first version (setup
after the release, no canary, no budget) recovered a real link-down on
silicon at 8 GT/s; this version is NOT hardware-proven. Observable on the
board, stimulus Link Disable on the root port for 1 s: the log shows
"link-down reset (SII_INT_1 LINK_REQ_RST_NOT), recovering", either "link
after reset: Gen3 x1" or "link not up 900 ms after the reset, programming
anyway", then "Root Port has been reset" and no "broken device, retraining"
line; LnkSta reads Gen3 x1 and LnkCtl2 Target Link Speed 3 afterwards; the
root port's COMMAND, bus numbers, LnkCtl, RtCtl and Root Error Command read
back as before the drop; SII_INT_MASK_1 reads 0x8 again; the NVMe answers
without a rescan. Whether "DBI setup lost to the controller's reset"
appears says whether a controller reset still follows the setup.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/Kconfig
@@ -261,6 +261,7 @@ config PCIE_SUN60I_A733
 	depends on ARCH_SUNXI || COMPILE_TEST
 	depends on PCI_MSI
 	select PCIE_DW_HOST
+	select PCI_HOST_COMMON
 	help
 	  Say Y here to enable PCIe controller support on Allwinner A733
 	  (sun60iw2) SoCs. The controller is a DesignWare core; this driver
diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-sun60i-a733.c b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-sun60i-a733.c
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-sun60i-a733.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-sun60i-a733.c
@@ -14,7 +14,9 @@
  *                                     [7:4] device type, reset value 4 = RC
  *   UDS + 0x0e00  SII_INT_MASK_0      1 passes an SII_INT_0 bit to the SII line
  *   UDS + 0x0e08  SII_INT_0           the root port's own event outputs, W1C
- *   UDS + 0x0e0c  SII_INT_1           bit0 SMLH link up, bit1 RDLH link up
+ *   UDS + 0x0e04  SII_INT_MASK_1      1 passes an SII_INT_1 bit to the SII line
+ *   UDS + 0x0e0c  SII_INT_1           bit0 SMLH link up, bit1 RDLH link up,
+ *                                     bit3 LINK_REQ_RST_NOT (link-down reset)
  *
  * All documented in the A733 UM V1.00, PCIe chapter 18.3, which tabulates the
  * whole User Defined Space. The DBI base itself is NOT where the UM prints it:
@@ -38,16 +40,19 @@
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/gpio/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/iopoll.h>
 #include <linux/irqchip/chained_irq.h>
 #include <linux/irqdomain.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/moduleparam.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
+#include <linux/pci.h>
 #include <linux/phy/phy.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/regulator/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/reset.h>
 
+#include "../pci-host-common.h"
 #include "pcie-designware.h"
 
 #define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LTSSM_CTRL		0x0c00
@@ -58,6 +63,13 @@
 #define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINK_STAT		0x0e0c
 #define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SMLH_LINK_UP		BIT(0)
 #define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_RDLH_LINK_UP		BIT(1)
+/*
+ * Reset request raised by a link-down or a received hot reset (UM V1.00
+ * 18.3.8.63). The controller then resets itself except for its sticky
+ * registers (the hot-reset entry under PCIE_LTSSM_ENABLE, 18.3.8.55).
+ * Same bit position in SII_INT_MASK_1.
+ */
+#define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINK_REQ_RST_NOT	BIT(3)
 
 /*
  * The System Information Interface: the DesignWare core's sideband outputs,
@@ -104,6 +116,17 @@
  */
 #define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_T_PVPERL_MS		100
 
+/* PCIe CEM 3.0 T_PERST: PERST# asserted for at least 100 us. */
+#define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_T_PERST_US		1000
+
+/*
+ * Link-down recoveries allowed per window before the handling gives up. A
+ * channel that fails equalization drops the link again right after
+ * each recovery; without a budget that is a loop.
+ */
+#define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINKDOWN_BUDGET	3
+#define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINKDOWN_WINDOW_S	60
+
 enum {
 	SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_AUX,
 	SUN60I_A733_PCIE_CLK_AXI_SLV,
@@ -137,6 +160,10 @@ struct sun60i_a733_pcie {
 	int			sii_irq;
 	u8			exp_cap;
 	u16			aer_cap;
+	struct work_struct	linkdown_work;
+	unsigned long		linkdown_window;
+	unsigned int		linkdown_count;
+	bool			linkdown_off;
 };
 
 static struct sun60i_a733_pcie *to_sun60i_a733_pcie(struct dw_pcie *pci)
@@ -270,6 +297,166 @@ static bool sun60i_a733_pcie_rp_pending(struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie)
 	return false;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Link-down handling.
+ *
+ * When the link drops, the controller resets itself except for its sticky
+ * registers: the UM's hot-reset (link-down) sequence under
+ * PCIE_LTSSM_ENABLE (18.3.8.55, printed p. 1221), announced by LINK_REQ_RST_NOT in SII_INT_1.
+ * Measured on 2026-09-21 after a link-down: the root port's Command, bus
+ * numbers, Link Control, Root Control and AER Root Error Command all read 0,
+ * while the port-logic registers (the MSI target at DBI + 0x820) kept their
+ * values. Nothing restored them, so the drive stayed unreachable and AER, PME
+ * and bwctrl stayed deaf until reboot.
+ *
+ * The recovery is the host-bridge link-down mechanism mainline has since
+ * gained (pci_host_handle_link_down() and pci_host_bridge::reset_root_port,
+ * backported in `pci-err-reset-root-ports-platform-specific` and `pci-host-common-link-down-handling-for-root-ports`): the drivers below the port are told the channel
+ * is frozen, the root port is reset through our reset_root_port() callback,
+ * the core restores the port's saved config space, waits for the device and
+ * lets the drivers restore theirs.
+ */
+static void sun60i_a733_pcie_linkdown_mask(struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie,
+					   bool unmask)
+{
+	unsigned long flags;
+	u32 val;
+
+	raw_spin_lock_irqsave(&pcie->sii_lock, flags);
+	val = readl(pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_1);
+	if (unmask) {
+		/*
+		 * Clear, then unmask: a request latched after the clear
+		 * raises the line at once.
+		 */
+		writel(SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINK_REQ_RST_NOT,
+		       pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINK_STAT);
+		val |= SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINK_REQ_RST_NOT;
+	} else {
+		val &= ~SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINK_REQ_RST_NOT;
+	}
+	writel(val, pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_1);
+	raw_spin_unlock_irqrestore(&pcie->sii_lock, flags);
+}
+
+static void sun60i_a733_pcie_linkdown_disarm(struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie)
+{
+	sun60i_a733_pcie_linkdown_mask(pcie, false);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Whether the controller has reset the root port's config space since it
+ * was last programmed. The witness is the secondary bus number: probe,
+ * dw_pcie_setup_rc() and the restore after a recovery all leave it non-zero,
+ * and the controller's reset clears it to 0 (measured 2026-09-21).
+ */
+static bool sun60i_a733_pcie_wiped(struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie)
+{
+	return !dw_pcie_readb_dbi(&pcie->pci, PCI_SECONDARY_BUS);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Arming clears the latch, and so erases a drop latched while disarmed.
+ * The glue's reset and the retrain that follows it need no software, so
+ * such a drop leaves a trained link over a wiped port. The canary catches
+ * it after the unmask and starts the recovery that the latch would have.
+ */
+static void sun60i_a733_pcie_linkdown_arm(struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie)
+{
+	if (pcie->linkdown_off)
+		return;
+
+	sun60i_a733_pcie_linkdown_mask(pcie, true);
+
+	if (sun60i_a733_pcie_wiped(pcie)) {
+		dev_warn(pcie->pci.dev,
+			 "root port wiped while link-down handling was disarmed, recovering\n");
+		sun60i_a733_pcie_linkdown_disarm(pcie);
+		queue_work(system_long_wq, &pcie->linkdown_work);
+	}
+}
+
+/*
+ * The root port's Data Link Layer Link Active bit, read through the DBI.
+ * Not sun60i_a733_pcie_link_up(): SII_INT_1's link-up bits are documented as
+ * write-1-to-clear, so whether they follow the link or only latch it is
+ * open, and this wait must see the link as it is now.
+ */
+static bool sun60i_a733_pcie_dl_active(struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie)
+{
+	return dw_pcie_readw_dbi(&pcie->pci, pcie->exp_cap + PCI_EXP_LNKSTA) &
+	       PCI_EXP_LNKSTA_DLLLA;
+}
+
+static int sun60i_a733_pcie_wait_dl_active(struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie)
+{
+	bool up;
+
+	return read_poll_timeout(sun60i_a733_pcie_dl_active, up, up,
+				 PCIE_LINK_WAIT_SLEEP_MS * USEC_PER_MSEC,
+				 PCIE_LINK_WAIT_MAX_RETRIES *
+				 PCIE_LINK_WAIT_SLEEP_MS * USEC_PER_MSEC,
+				 false, pcie);
+}
+
+static void sun60i_a733_pcie_log_link(struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie,
+				      const char *when)
+{
+	u16 sta = dw_pcie_readw_dbi(&pcie->pci, pcie->exp_cap + PCI_EXP_LNKSTA);
+
+	dev_info(pcie->pci.dev, "%s: Gen%u x%u, DLLLA %d\n", when,
+		 FIELD_GET(PCI_EXP_LNKSTA_CLS, sta),
+		 FIELD_GET(PCI_EXP_LNKSTA_NLW, sta),
+		 !!(sta & PCI_EXP_LNKSTA_DLLLA));
+}
+
+static void sun60i_a733_pcie_linkdown_work(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie =
+		container_of(work, struct sun60i_a733_pcie, linkdown_work);
+	struct pci_bus *bus = pcie->pci.pp.bridge->bus;
+	struct device *dev = pcie->pci.dev;
+	struct pci_dev *port;
+
+	if (pcie->linkdown_off)
+		return;
+
+	if (!pcie->linkdown_count ||
+	    time_after(jiffies, pcie->linkdown_window +
+				SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINKDOWN_WINDOW_S * HZ)) {
+		pcie->linkdown_window = jiffies;
+		pcie->linkdown_count = 0;
+	}
+	if (++pcie->linkdown_count > SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINKDOWN_BUDGET) {
+		pcie->linkdown_off = true;
+		dev_err(dev,
+			"%d link-down recoveries within %d s; link-down handling off until reboot\n",
+			SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINKDOWN_BUDGET,
+			SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINKDOWN_WINDOW_S);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	dev_warn(dev,
+		 "link-down reset (SII_INT_1 LINK_REQ_RST_NOT), recovering (%u of %d in %d s)\n",
+		 pcie->linkdown_count, SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINKDOWN_BUDGET,
+		 SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINKDOWN_WINDOW_S);
+
+	for_each_pci_bridge(port, bus)
+		if (pci_pcie_type(port) == PCI_EXP_TYPE_ROOT_PORT)
+			pci_host_handle_link_down(port);
+
+	/*
+	 * reset_root_port() re-arms. This covers a recovery that never reached
+	 * it; a link that stayed down stays disarmed, or the request would
+	 * fire again at once, and says so. Arming checks the canary.
+	 */
+	if (sun60i_a733_pcie_dl_active(pcie))
+		sun60i_a733_pcie_linkdown_arm(pcie);
+	else
+		dev_err(dev,
+			"link still down after recovery; link-down handling disarmed\n");
+}
+
 static void sun60i_a733_pcie_sii_handler(struct irq_desc *desc)
 {
 	struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie = irq_desc_get_handler_data(desc);
@@ -301,6 +488,18 @@ static void sun60i_a733_pcie_sii_handler(struct irq_desc *desc)
 					     pass);
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A link-down reset. Disarm and hand it to process context; the
+	 * reset of the root port that follows re-arms it once the link is
+	 * back (sun60i_a733_pcie_linkdown_arm()).
+	 */
+	if (readl(pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINK_STAT) &
+	    readl(pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_1) &
+	    SUN60I_A733_PCIE_LINK_REQ_RST_NOT) {
+		sun60i_a733_pcie_linkdown_disarm(pcie);
+		queue_work(system_long_wq, &pcie->linkdown_work);
+	}
+
 	chained_irq_exit(chip, desc);
 }
 
@@ -352,6 +551,138 @@ static const struct irq_domain_ops sun60i_a733_pcie_intx_domain_ops = {
 	.xlate = irq_domain_xlate_onecell,
 };
 
+/*
+ * Capabilities the root port advertises and must not:
+ *
+ *  - MSI and MSI-X: see the root port's event interrupt above, it runs on
+ *    INTx.
+ *  - VPD is advertised and nothing serves it. The DesignWare core only
+ *    pulses cfg_vpd_int (SII_INT_0 bit 0, UM V1.00 18.3.8.62) and leaves
+ *    the transfer and the VPD flag to an application the A733 does not
+ *    have, so every access times out in pci_vpd_wait() with "VPD access
+ *    failed". Unlinked, the port says what is true: it has no VPD.
+ *
+ * Idempotent: a capability already unlinked is not found again. After a
+ * link-down reset it runs once more, because whether that reset restores
+ * the list pointers the DBI wrote is not documented; if it does, the line
+ * below says so, which is the measurement.
+ */
+static void sun60i_a733_pcie_hide_caps(struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie,
+				       bool again)
+{
+	static const u8 caps[] = { PCI_CAP_ID_MSI, PCI_CAP_ID_MSIX,
+				   PCI_CAP_ID_VPD };
+	struct dw_pcie *pci = &pcie->pci;
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(caps); i++) {
+		if (again && dw_pcie_find_capability(pci, caps[i]))
+			dev_info(pci->dev,
+				 "capability %#04x is back after the reset, unlinking it again\n",
+				 caps[i]);
+		dw_pcie_remove_capability(pci, caps[i]);
+	}
+}
+
+/*
+ * pci_host_bridge::reset_root_port. Reached from every reset of the root
+ * port's secondary bus: link-down recovery above, AER fatal recovery
+ * (aer_root_reset()), and a bus reset requested for the drive. A plain
+ * Secondary Bus Reset is no use here: the link drop it causes resets the
+ * controller, Bridge Control and bus numbers included, halfway through.
+ *
+ * So one sequence for all of them: hold the LTSSM, pulse PERST#, release
+ * the LTSSM, wait for the link, then do the DBI setup probe did -- the
+ * capability unlinks and dw_pcie_setup_rc() -- and check the canary.
+ *
+ * The setup comes after the release, not while the LTSSM is held. Holding
+ * app_ltssm_enable here is the UM's first window of a hot reset
+ * (18.3.8.55): a DBI read, then app_ltssm_enable = 1, then the reset
+ * of the controller starts. Writes made in that window come before that
+ * reset, which by the UM takes them. Measured 2026-09-22 with the setup
+ * written in that window: the link came back only through the PCI core's
+ * 2.5 GT/s retrain (pcie_failed_link_retrain()) and stayed at 2.5 GT/s;
+ * written after the release it came back at 8 GT/s. The UM's second window,
+ * a hold applied combinationally at the falling edge of core_rst_n,
+ * is not reachable from software. The upstream rockchip and qcom callbacks
+ * write the setup before the release because they reset the core
+ * themselves first; this one does not, the controller does.
+ *
+ * dw_pcie_setup_rc() ends with a directed speed change, which is what takes
+ * the link from 2.5 GT/s back to 8 GT/s, also when it is written before
